## 1. What is Routing?

Routing is the process of selecting the best path for
network traffic to travel from source to destination
across one or more networks.

A router makes forwarding decisions based on:
- Destination IP address in packet header
- Routing table — map of known networks and how to reach them
- Routing protocol metrics — cost, hop count, bandwidth

---

## 2. Types of Routing

### Static Routing
- Routes manually configured by administrator
- Does not change automatically if network topology changes
- Suitable for small networks with simple topology
- Lower CPU and bandwidth overhead
- Security benefit — no routing protocol traffic to intercept

! Static route syntax
R1(config)# ip route [destination] [mask] [next-hop or exit-interface]
! Example — route to 10.0.0.0/8 via 192.168.1.254
R1(config)# ip route 10.0.0.0 255.0.0.0 192.168.1.254
! Default route — matches all destinations
R1(config)# ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 203.0.113.2
! Verify
R1# show ip route static

### Dynamic Routing
- Routers automatically discover routes and share with neighbours
- Adapts automatically to network topology changes
- Suitable for larger more complex networks
- Requires more CPU and bandwidth
- Routing protocol runs continuously

### Administrative Distance
When multiple routing sources know about the same network
administrative distance determines which is preferred:

| Source | Administrative Distance |
|--------|------------------------|
| Connected interface | 0 |
| Static route | 1 |
| OSPF | 110 |
| RIP | 120 |
| Unknown | 255 — unusable |

Lower administrative distance = more trusted = preferred

