## 3. RIP — Routing Information Protocol

RIP is a distance vector routing protocol — routers share
their entire routing table with directly connected neighbours.

### RIP Characteristics
- Metric: Hop count — number of routers to destination
- Maximum hop count: 15 — destinations beyond 15 hops
  are unreachable
- Updates: Broadcasts full routing table every 30 seconds
- Convergence: Slow — can take minutes after topology change
- Versions: RIPv1 (classful) and RIPv2 (classless — supports CIDR)

### RIP Configuration

R1(config)# router rip
R1(config-router)# version 2
R1(config-router)# no auto-summary
R1(config-router)# network 192.168.1.0
R1(config-router)# network 10.0.0.0
R1(config-router)# passive-interface GigabitEthernet0/1
R1(config-router)# exit
! Verify
R1# show ip protocols
R1# show ip route rip
R1# debug ip rip                    ! Show RIP updates in real time
R1# undebug all                     ! Stop debugging

### RIP Security Issues
- No authentication by default — routes can be injected
- Broadcasts routing table — information disclosure
- Slow convergence creates window for routing attacks
- Maximum 15 hops limits scalability

### RIP Authentication

R1(config)# key chain RIP_KEYS
R1(config-keychain)# key 1
R1(config-keychain-key)# key-string MyR1pK3y
R1(config-keychain-key)# exit
R1(config-keychain)# exit
R1(config)# interface GigabitEthernet0/0
R1(config-if)# ip rip authentication mode md5
R1(config-if)# ip rip authentication key-chain RIP_KEYS

