## 5. BGP — Border Gateway Protocol

BGP is the routing protocol of the internet — used between
different autonomous systems (AS) — different organisations
or ISPs.

### BGP Characteristics
- Path vector protocol — tracks AS path to prevent loops
- Highly configurable — extensive policy control
- Slow convergence by design — stability over speed
- Used by ISPs and large enterprises
- Not typically configured by entry-level administrators
- Understanding BGP important for cybersecurity —
  BGP hijacking is a significant internet-level threat

### BGP Security Issues
- BGP hijacking — malicious or accidental announcement
  of wrong routes causes traffic redirection
- No built-in authentication in original BGP
- RPKI (Resource Public Key Infrastructure) helps
  validate route origins
- BGP route leaks can cause widespread internet disruption

